menu "Voice assistant configuration"

    menu "Network"

        # Two slots. Slot 1 is normally the venue/lab network, slot 2 a phone
        # hotspot. Both are tried in turn on every reconnect, so the device
        # works in either place without a recompile.

        config WIFI_SSID
            string "Network 1 - WiFi SSID"
            default "YOUR_WIFI_SSID"
            help
                Name of the 2.4 GHz WiFi network. The ESP32-S3 has no 5 GHz
                radio, so a phone hotspot must be set to 2.4 GHz (iPhone:
                "Maximise Compatibility"; Android: set the AP band to 2.4 GHz).

                Leave this as YOUR_WIFI_SSID to disable slot 1.

        config WIFI_PASSWORD
            string "Network 1 - WiFi password"
            default "YOUR_WIFI_PASSWORD"

        config WIFI_SSID_2
            string "Network 2 - WiFi SSID (e.g. phone hotspot)"
            default "YOUR_WIFI_SSID_2"
            help
                Optional second network. Leave as YOUR_WIFI_SSID_2 to disable.

                This is the slot to use for a phone hotspot: fill in the
                hotspot's SSID and password here and the device will fall back
                to it whenever the first network is unavailable. On a phone,
                Android often prefixes the SSID with the device name (for
                example "Pixel_1234"), so check the exact spelling on the
                phone rather than assuming.

        config WIFI_PASSWORD_2
            string "Network 2 - WiFi password"
            default "YOUR_WIFI_PASSWORD_2"

        config WIFI_CANDIDATE_MAX
            int "Maximum number of candidate networks"
            range 1 4
            default 2
            help
                Upper bound on how many SSID slots the WiFi manager keeps.
                Must be at least as large as the number of slots filled in
                above, and is used to size some stack arrays.

        config WIFI_MAX_RETRY
            int "Max connection retries before giving up (legacy, 0 = never)"
            range 0 100
            default 0
            help
                Superseded by the exponential-backoff reconnect loop. Kept
                because the value still appears in sdkconfig from older
                builds; 0 means "never give up", which is the intended
                behaviour for an unattended device at a venue.

    endmenu

    menu "DeepSeek"

        config DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL
            string "API base URL"
            default "https://api.deepseek.com"
            help
                Base URL of the OpenAI-compatible endpoint. The client
                appends "/chat/completions" (unless the value already ends
                with it, so pasting the full URL also works).

                Official service:
                    https://api.deepseek.com

                Third-party relay / proxy (中间站), for example:
                    https://router.shengsuanyun.com/api/v1

                A relay hands out keys that ONLY work against its own
                host, so leaving this at the official URL while using a
                relay key gives HTTP 401.

                The HTTPS certificate must be in the ESP-IDF CA bundle.
                That covers public CAs; a relay using a self-signed cert
                will not validate.

        config DEEPSEEK_SEND_THINKING_PARAMS
            bool "Send thinking / reasoning_effort parameters"
            default y
            help
                The official DeepSeek API accepts "thinking" and
                "reasoning_effort"; disabling them keeps the spoken reply
                short and low-latency.

                Some relays and older OpenAI-compatible servers reject
                unknown request fields with HTTP 400. Turn this OFF if the
                request fails with 400 and the body mentions an unknown or
                unexpected parameter.

                Has no effect on the reply text itself either way - it only
                controls whether the model is asked to skip reasoning.

        config DEEPSEEK_API_KEY
            string "API key"
            default "sk-REPLACE_ME"
            help
                Key from the service selected by DEEPSEEK_BASE_URL.

                Official keys look like "sk-..." (about 35 characters).
                Relay keys vary in length and often do NOT start with
                "sk-".

                Stored in sdkconfig, which is git-ignored.

        config DEEPSEEK_MODEL
            string "Model name"
            default "deepseek-flash"
            help
                Official service:
                    deepseek-flash  = DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash (fast, cheap)
                    deepseek-v4-pro = DeepSeek-V4-Pro

                A relay may use different names - it usually publishes its
                own list (often at <base>/models). The name is passed
                through unchanged, so use whatever the relay documents.

        config DEEPSEEK_SYSTEM_PROMPT
            string "System prompt (persona)"
            default "你是一个友善的智能语音助手，回答要简短口语化，控制在50字以内，不要使用markdown格式。"

        config DEEPSEEK_MAX_TOKENS
            int "Max reply tokens"
            range 32 4096
            default 200
            help
                Keep this small - the reply is spoken aloud, long answers
                are tedious to listen to.

    endmenu

    menu "iFlytek (讯飞)"

        config IFLYTEK_APP_ID
            string "AppID"
            default "REPLACE_ME_APPID"

        config IFLYTEK_API_KEY
            string "APIKey"
            default "REPLACE_ME_APIKEY"

        config IFLYTEK_API_SECRET
            string "APISecret"
            default "REPLACE_ME_APISECRET"

        config IFLYTEK_STT_HOST
            string "STT host"
            default "iat-api.xfyun.cn"
            help
                iat-api.xfyun.cn  = 语音听写 (short, one-shot)
                ws-api.xfyun.cn   = 语音转写 (long-form)

        config IFLYTEK_TTS_HOST
            string "TTS host"
            default "tts-api.xfyun.cn"

        config IFLYTEK_TTS_VOICE
            string "TTS voice"
            default "xiaoyan"
            help
                xiaoyan  = 讯飞小燕 (female, default)
                aisjiuxu = 许久
                aisxping = 小萍
                aisjinger= 小婧

        config IFLYTEK_TTS_SPEED
            int "TTS speed (0-100)"
            range 0 100
            default 50

    endmenu

    menu "SiliconFlow (硅基流动)"

        # Bearer-token + plain HTTP, modelled on the OpenAI audio API. That is
        # the reason this backend exists: it needs ONE key that is handed out on
        # signup, where iFlytek needs AppID + APIKey + APISecret fed into an
        # HMAC-SHA256 signature over a WebSocket URL.
        #
        # Because the endpoints follow the OpenAI shape, swapping to another
        # compatible provider (or a self-hosted FunASR/sherpa-onnx server that
        # exposes the same two routes) is only a base-URL and model-name change.

        config SILICONFLOW_BASE_URL
            string "API base URL"
            default "https://api.siliconflow.cn/v1"
            help
                Note the ".cn" - SiliconFlow runs separate cn and com hosts and
                a key issued on one is rejected by the other.

                The path is optional: "https://api.siliconflow.cn" and
                "https://api.siliconflow.cn/v1" are both accepted, and pasting
                the complete endpoint URL by mistake also works.

        config SILICONFLOW_API_KEY
            string "API key"
            default "REPLACE_ME_SILICONFLOW_KEY"
            help
                From the SiliconFlow console (API 密钥 page). A single key
                covers both the transcription and the speech endpoints.

        config SILICONFLOW_STT_MODEL
            string "Speech-to-text model"
            default "FunAudioLLM/SenseVoiceSmall"
            help
                The transcription endpoint accepts exactly two values:
                    FunAudioLLM/SenseVoiceSmall  (SenseVoice, strong Chinese)
                    TeleAI/TeleSpeechASR
                SenseVoiceSmall is the FunASR-family model and the default.

                Anything else - including Qwen3-ASR - is rejected, because the
                model whitelist for this route is maintained separately from the
                text-model catalogue.

        config SILICONFLOW_TTS_MODEL
            string "Text-to-speech model"
            default "FunAudioLLM/CosyVoice2-0.5B"
            help
                FunAudioLLM/CosyVoice2-0.5B  (Chinese + dialects, recommended)
                fishaudio/fish-speech-1.5    (12 languages; note that the
                                              provider bills this one against
                                              topped-up balance only)

        config SILICONFLOW_TTS_VOICE
            string "TTS voice"
            default "FunAudioLLM/CosyVoice2-0.5B:anna"
            help
                The voice name must be prefixed with the model name, or the
                service rejects it. System presets:

                    female: anna (calm), bella (passionate),
                            claire (gentle), diana (cheerful)
                    male:   alex (steady), benjamin (deep),
                            charles (magnetic), david (cheerful)

                Example: FunAudioLLM/CosyVoice2-0.5B:anna

        config SILICONFLOW_TTS_SPEED
            string "TTS speed"
            default "1.0"
            help
                Float between 0.25 and 4.0. A string rather than a number
                because the JSON body needs the decimal point preserved.

        config SILICONFLOW_TTS_GAIN
            string "TTS gain (dB)"
            default "0.0"
            help
                Float between -10 and 10. Raise it if playback is too quiet for
                the MAX98357A and speaker; the default leaves the level alone.

    endmenu

    menu "Speech backends"

        # Both backends stay compiled and switchable so the iFlytek path can be
        # restored without editing code if a key or the free quota runs out.

        choice STT_BACKEND
            prompt "Speech-to-text backend"

            config STT_BACKEND_IFLYTEK
                bool "iFlytek 语音听写"
                help
                    WebSocket + HMAC-SHA256 signature. Needs three credentials.

            config STT_BACKEND_SILICONFLOW
                bool "SiliconFlow (SenseVoice)"
                help
                    Plain HTTPS POST of a WAV file, authenticated with a single
                    Bearer token. Fewer moving parts and no signature code path
                    to get wrong.

        endchoice

        config TTS_LOCAL_ENABLE
            bool "Try a local OpenAI-compatible TTS server before the cloud"
            default n
            help
                The local Qwen3-TTS service speaks the same /v1/audio/speech
                shape as SiliconFlow, so the same client code targets it - only
                the URL, key and voice differ. Measured on an RTX 5060 Laptop:
                first audio in 0.42-0.56 s, against 2.5-14 s from the cloud.

                When it fails (laptop asleep, off the network) the configured
                cloud backend is still tried, so this is an accelerator rather
                than a hard dependency.

        config TTS_LOCAL_BASE_URL
            string "Local TTS base URL"
            depends on TTS_LOCAL_ENABLE
            default "http://192.168.1.100:8000/v1"
            help
                Host:port of tts-server/app.py. The device must be on the same
                LAN - a laptop on campus wifi cannot be reached by a board on a
                phone hotspot.

        config TTS_LOCAL_VOICE
            string "Local TTS voice (speaker id)"
            depends on TTS_LOCAL_ENABLE
            default "sohee"

        choice TTS_BACKEND
            prompt "Text-to-speech backend"

            config TTS_BACKEND_IFLYTEK
                bool "iFlytek 在线语音合成"

            config TTS_BACKEND_SILICONFLOW
                bool "SiliconFlow (CosyVoice2)"
                help
                    Requests raw PCM from the service, so the bytes that arrive
                    are playable as-is with no container to strip.

        endchoice

    endmenu

    menu "Audio"

        config AUDIO_SAMPLE_RATE
            int "Sample rate (Hz)"
            range 8000 48000
            default 16000
            help
                16000 is required by the iFlytek STT service and is also
                what WakeNet expects. Do not change unless you know why.

        config AUDIO_MIC_SHIFT
            int "Microphone right-shift (bigger = less gain)"
            range 8 18
            default 14
            help
                The INMP441 is 24-bit and sits well below full scale at
                conversational distance, so the sample is shifted right to fit
                into 16 bits. Shift 14 is 4x gain, 13 is 8x.

                Measured on this build: at 8x the transcriber returned an empty
                string for every utterance, at 4x it recognises speech. More
                gain is not better - it clips the peaks and the waveform that
                reaches the model is no longer intelligible.

                The shift is followed by saturation, never a plain cast: an
                overflowing cast wraps the sign bit and turns loud syllables
                into broadband noise.

        config AUDIO_OUT_PREROLL_MS
            int "Pre-roll before playback starts (ms)"
            range 0 3000
            default 1200
            help
                How much audio to hold before the first sample is written.

                The local TTS server produces audio at ~2x realtime, but it does
                not arrive evenly: measured delivery stalls on a phone hotspot
                reach ~600 ms, which drained the DMA and was audible as a gap
                mid-sentence. Buffering this much first turns uneven arrival
                into a non-event.

                Costs latency: the first word is heard this much later. 0
                disables the pre-roll and plays as data arrives.

        config AUDIO_OUT_SAMPLE_RATE
            int "Playback sample rate (Hz)"
            range 8000 48000
            default 16000
            help
                Deliberately separate from AUDIO_SAMPLE_RATE.

                The amplifier has its own I2S bus, so it can run at a different
                rate from the microphone. The local Qwen3-TTS server produces
                24 kHz natively; asking it for 16 kHz would force a resample on
                every streamed chunk, which is audible at chunk boundaries.
                Set this to 24000 when TTS_LOCAL_ENABLE is on.

        config AUDIO_RECORD_SECONDS
            int "Max recording length (seconds)"
            range 1 30
            default 6
            help
                Hard cap per utterance. Recording also stops early when the
                silence detector trips.

        config AUDIO_SILENCE_MS
            int "Silence duration that ends recording (ms)"
            range 300 3000
            default 1000
            help
                If the signal stays below the silence threshold for this
                long, recording stops and the utterance is sent for
                recognition.

        config AUDIO_SILENCE_THRESHOLD
            int "Silence threshold (RMS, 16-bit scale)"
            range 50 20000
            default 600
            help
                RMS below this counts as silence. Raise it in a noisy room,
                lower it if the device cuts you off mid-sentence.

    endmenu

    menu "Wake word"

        choice WAKE_BACKEND
            prompt "Wake word backend"

            config WAKE_BACKEND_VAD
                bool "Energy VAD (no extra components)"
                help
                    Always available. Triggers on any sufficiently loud
                    sound, so it is easy to trigger by accident. Useful for
                    bring-up and testing before wake-word assets are in place.

            config WAKE_BACKEND_WAKENET
                bool "ESP-SR WakeNet (offline neural wake word)"
                help
                    Real wake word detection. Requires the esp-sr managed
                    component and a model partition (several MB of flash).
                    Far more accurate, and the intended configuration.

        endchoice

        config WAKE_TRIGGER_RMS
            int "VAD trigger RMS"
            range 200 30000
            default 2500
            depends on WAKE_BACKEND_VAD

        config WAKENET_MODEL
            string "WakeNet model name"
            default "wn9_hilexin"
            depends on WAKE_BACKEND_WAKENET
            help
                wn9_hilexin  = "你好小鑫"
                wn9_nihaoxiaozhi = "你好小智"
                Check the ESP-SR docs for the model that ships with your
                IDF version.

    endmenu

    menu "Pins"

        # Defaults chosen to avoid every pin that IDF reserves or warns about:
        #   0     BOOT strapping
        #   3     JTAG (also UART0 RX, the console)
        #   19/20 USB-Serial-JTAG (and USB-OTG on some parts)
        #   26-32 SPI flash / octal PSRAM
        #   33-37 octal PSRAM (S3 with embedded PSRAM)
        #   39-42 JTAG
        #   43/44 UART0 TX/RX, the console
        #   45/46 strapping (VDD_SPI / boot mode)
        # GPIO4-18 and 21 are safe for general use; the four below are all in
        # that range and are not otherwise spoken for on a typical S3 board.

        config PIN_I2S_BCLK
            int "I2S bit clock (microphone bus only)"
            range 0 48
            default 4

        config PIN_I2S_WS
            int "I2S word select / LRCK (microphone bus only)"
            range 0 48
            default 5

        config PIN_I2S_MIC_DATA
            int "I2S data from INMP441 (SD)"
            range 0 48
            default 6

        config PIN_I2S_AMP_DATA
            int "I2S data to MAX98357A (DIN)"
            range 0 48
            default 7

        config TTS_SELFTEST
            bool "Synthesise and play one sentence at boot"
            default y
            help
                Measures the active TTS backend end to end without needing a
                successful speech recognition first, and plays the result so the
                analogue path can be judged at the same time. Costs one TTS
                request per boot - turn it off once the backend is trusted.
        config PIN_I2S_AMP_BCLK
            int "MAX98357A bit clock (its own clock, not shared)"
            range 0 48
            default 12
            help
                The amplifier runs its own I2S bus. Sharing BCLK/WS with the
                microphone means two peripherals drive the same pins, and the
                slave side then has no guaranteed phase relationship between
                its data output and that clock - audible as hiss on top of a
                pure tone. Two spare pins are cheaper than that.

        config PIN_I2S_AMP_WS
            int "MAX98357A word select / LRCK (its own clock)"
            range 0 48
            default 13

        config PIN_AMP_ENABLE
            int "MAX98357A SD / enable pin (-1 to disable)"
            range -1 48
            default 15
            help
                Drive high to un-mute the amplifier. Set to -1 if SD is
                hard-wired high on your board.

        config PIN_LED
            int "On-board addressable RGB LED"
            range 0 48
            default 48

    endmenu

    config BOOT_BUTTON_TRIGGER
        bool "Press BOOT (GPIO0) to start a turn manually"
        default y
        help
            Useful while bringing the device up: short-press BOOT to force
            a recording without waiting for the wake word.

    menu "Low power (battery / weak USB port)"

        # See main/power_save.c for the full reasoning, and be aware that on
        # THIS board the whole menu has been measured as having no effect:
        # the reset lands inside esp_wifi_start(), and the runtime TX power
        # API cannot be called until esp_wifi_start() has returned, so it is
        # never reached. It is kept for boards that are less far short.

        config LOW_POWER_MODE
            bool "Enable low power mode"
            default n
            help
                Reduces the radio's peak current so the board may survive on
                a battery bank or a weak USB port.

                MEASURED: NO EFFECT ON THIS BOARD. Instrumenting the station
                startup showed the brownout lands inside esp_wifi_start():

                    [4/6] esp_wifi_init (RF calibration)   <- completes
                    [5/6] esp_wifi_init returned           <- so calibration
                                                              is fine
                    [6/6] esp_wifi_start                   <- never returns

                esp_wifi_set_max_tx_power() is documented to require
                esp_wifi_start() to have completed, so the call this option
                depends on is unreachable here. Why the option is still
                present: on a supply that is only slightly short it should
                help, and the diagnostic value of a negative result is real.

                It does NOT fix the supply either way. It lowers the current
                demand; it cannot raise the voltage. See
                BROWNOUT-DIAGNOSIS.md and the README.

                The main effect, where it does apply, is a much lower WiFi
                transmit power, which cuts range. Fine for a phone hotspot
                on the same table.

        config LOW_POWER_TX_POWER_DBM
            int "WiFi transmit power cap (dBm)"
            depends on LOW_POWER_MODE
            range 2 20
            default 2
            help
                Applied at runtime via esp_wifi_set_max_tx_power().

                This is the lever with real headroom: the driver default
                comes from CONFIG_ESP_PHY_MAX_WIFI_TX_POWER, whose Kconfig
                floor is 10 dBm, but this API accepts values down to 2 dBm.
                Transmit burst current scales roughly with linear output
                power, so 2 dBm is several times less current than 10 dBm at
                exactly the moment the rail collapses.

                Range guidance: 2 dBm (~1.6 mW) is good for a hotspot on the
                same desk. Use 8-10 dBm if you need a few metres.

                Note it only takes effect if esp_wifi_start() returns, which
                it does not on this board - see LOW_POWER_MODE above.

        config LOW_POWER_WIFI_MAX_MODEM
            bool "Aggressive WiFi sleep (MAX_MODEM)"
            depends on LOW_POWER_MODE
            default n
            help
                WIFI_PS_MAX_MODEM sleeps for listen_interval beacon periods
                instead of waking on every DTIM, which draws less. The cost
                is latency and, on a busy or lossy AP, a real risk of missed
                beacons being read as the AP disappearing - which drops the
                link.

                Leave this off unless MIN_MODEM still is not enough; losing
                the connection is worse than a higher idle draw here.

        config LOW_POWER_CPU_MAX_MHZ
            int "Maximum CPU frequency (MHz)"
            depends on LOW_POWER_MODE
            range 80 240
            default 160
            help
                Requires CONFIG_PM_ENABLE. Lowering the CPU ceiling cuts the
                compute share of the current budget, but NOT the radio burst,
                which is what actually trips the detector - measured on its
                own as ineffective. Kept because it stacks with the others.

        config LOW_POWER_CPU_MIN_MHZ
            int "Minimum CPU frequency (MHz)"
            depends on LOW_POWER_MODE
            range 80 240
            default 80
            help
                Frequency used when nothing holds a power-management lock.
                Requires CONFIG_PM_ENABLE.

    endmenu

    menu "Servo (panel switch actuator)"

        # A concealed 86-type panel switch is a mechanical rocker with a
        # snap-over action: it needs real torque and tens of degrees of travel.
        # Two consequences for this project:
        #   * the servo must be powered from the 5V rail on its own branch -
        #     it can draw 0.7-1.5 A when starting or when stalled against the
        #     switch, which this board's supply cannot absorb (DIAGNOSIS-POWER.md)
        #   * the endpoints below must be measured on the bench, not assumed;
        #     a servo buzzing against a mechanical stop draws stall current
        #     continuously.

        config SERVO_ENABLE
            bool "Enable the servo output"
            default n
            help
                Drives an RC servo (SG90 / MG90S class) from a hardware PWM
                channel, for throwing a panel switch.

                The servo's signal wire goes straight to the GPIO - there is no
                driver IC to add, and no level shifter: the servo's signal input
                is high impedance and accepts 3.3V logic.

                What this option does NOT do is power the servo. That has to
                come from the 5V rail on a separate branch with its own bulk
                capacitor, or moving the servo will reset the board.

        config SERVO_GPIO
            int "Servo signal GPIO"
            depends on SERVO_ENABLE
            default 5
            range 1 48
            help
                GPIO5 by choice. Avoid 0/3/45/46 (strapping), 19/20 (native
                USB), 26-37 (flash and PSRAM) and 43/44 (console UART).

        config SERVO_FREQ_HZ
            int "PWM frame rate (Hz)"
            depends on SERVO_ENABLE
            default 50
            range 40 400
            help
                Standard analogue servos expect 50 Hz (a 20 ms frame). Digital
                servos accept up to 300 Hz and run smoother, but if you are not
                sure the part is digital, leave this at 50.

        config SERVO_PULSE_MIN_US
            int "Pulse width at the minimum angle (us)"
            depends on SERVO_ENABLE
            default 1000
            range 300 2500

        config SERVO_PULSE_MAX_US
            int "Pulse width at the maximum angle (us)"
            depends on SERVO_ENABLE
            default 2000
            range 300 2500

        config SERVO_ANGLE_MIN_DEG
            int "Minimum usable angle (deg)"
            depends on SERVO_ENABLE
            default 20
            range 0 180
            help
                The safe mechanical endpoint, not 0. Most 9 g servos buzz or
                stall somewhere below 10 deg and above 170 deg; set this to the
                smallest angle the arm reaches without straining.

        config SERVO_ANGLE_MAX_DEG
            int "Maximum usable angle (deg)"
            depends on SERVO_ENABLE
            default 160
            range 0 180

        config SERVO_POS_A_DEG
            int "Switch position A (deg)"
            depends on SERVO_ENABLE
            default 45
            range 0 180
            help
                One of the switch's two stable states, in servo degrees.
                Found by testing: command angles until the switch snaps over,
                then use that angle with a couple of degrees of margin.

        config SERVO_POS_B_DEG
            int "Switch position B (deg)"
            depends on SERVO_ENABLE
            default 115
            range 0 180

        config SERVO_STEP_DEG
            int "Ramp step size (deg)"
            depends on SERVO_ENABLE
            default 2
            range 1 30
            help
                A move is walked in steps of this size. Smaller = slower and
                gentler on the supply; larger = snappier but a bigger current
                spike at the start of the move.

        config SERVO_STEP_MS
            int "Delay between ramp steps (ms)"
            depends on SERVO_ENABLE
            default 20
            range 1 200
            help
                20 ms per 2 deg step means roughly 1.8 s for a 90 deg throw.

        config SERVO_TEST_MODE
            bool "TEST MODE: servo bench mode (no WiFi, no audio)"
            depends on SERVO_ENABLE
            default n
            help
                Starts the console and the servo, then stops: the radio and the
                audio path are never initialised.

                Why this is worth having: with the assistant firmware running,
                this board resets about 1.4 times a second during radio startup,
                which makes it impossible to watch a servo move and judge
                whether the arm is stalling against the switch. In this mode the
                board is electrically quiet, so the servo can be exercised at
                leisure with the console commands:

                    a / b   move to the two switch positions
                    t       toggle between them
                    , / .   jog 5 deg down / up  <- use these to find the stops
                    p       release (no holding torque, no standing current)
                    s       show the current configuration

                The angles you discover here are what CONFIG_SERVO_ANGLE_MIN_DEG,
                _MAX_DEG, POS_A_DEG and POS_B_DEG should be set to.

                Turn it off to get the assistant back.

    endmenu

    menu "Radio bring-up (current shedding at the reset point)"

        # MEASURED 2026-10-02 with the board on USB: the rail collapses much
        # earlier than these notes used to claim. Step markers showed the
        # reset landing inside esp_wifi_init(), immediately after the WiFi
        # library prints "Allocate MAC register memory" - the same place on
        # every boot of a 20 s window (10 brownouts in 20 s, highest app
        # timestamp 762 ms). esp_wifi_start() is never reached at all on this
        # supply, so every runtime TX-power lever is unreachable by
        # construction, which is why none of them ever helped.
        #
        # What remains is to make the radio's power-up window as cheap as
        # possible: fewer things switching at the same moment, and a lower CPU
        # clock while the MAC/PHY domain comes up. That is what this menu does.

        config BRINGUP_LOW_CPU
            bool "Drop the CPU to 80 MHz while the radio powers up"
            default n
            help
                The S3 comes out of reset at 240 MHz. Dynamic frequency
                scaling (CONFIG_PM_ENABLE) is already on for the runtime, but
                the boot path reaches esp_wifi_init() at full clock, so the
                radio power-up spike rides on top of the compute current at
                its maximum.

                This caps the CPU at 80 MHz just before esp_wifi_init() and
                restores the normal ceiling once the radio is up - roughly a
                third of the compute current at the moment the rail collapses.

                It cannot raise the voltage, so on a supply this far short it
                may well change nothing. It is still the cheapest thing to
                try: one build, no soldering.

        config BRINGUP_QUIET_PERIPHERALS
            bool "Silence I2S and the LED during radio bring-up"
            default n
            help
                Stops both I2S channels (microphone and amplifier bit clocks)
                and blanks the RGB LED while the radio powers up, then restores
                them once the station has started.

                Together these are a real part of the current at the spike:
                the I2S bit clock runs continuously even with no audio, and a
                bright WS2812 draws tens of milliamps by itself. Silence also
                settles the amplifier question - GPIO18 is driven low before
                the radio comes up, so the MAX98357A cannot be what tips the
                rail over.

                Costs a few milliseconds of extra boot time. If the audio or
                the LED fails to come back, the log says so explicitly.

        config BRINGUP_TRACE
            bool "BROWNOUT DIAGNOSTIC: verbose bring-up markers with timing"
            default n
            help
                The fault has already been narrowed to esp_wifi_init(), which
                is a 100+ ms window inside a WiFi library blob. This adds a
                finer layer of markers around that window, each carrying the
                elapsed time and the free internal heap, so a change that
                moves the reset point by a few milliseconds is still visible.

                Turn it off for normal use: it prints several lines per boot.

    endmenu

    # ---------------------------------------------------------------------
    # Shared I2C bus settings.
    #
    # These live outside the rail monitor menu on purpose: the bus is shared
    # furniture, and the servo bench mode also probes it (otherwise a dark
    # OLED there is indistinguishable from a wiring fault). Entries inside a
    # menu inherit that menu's conditions, so putting them here is also what
    # makes them visible in every mode.
    # ---------------------------------------------------------------------

    config I2C_SDA_GPIO
        int "Shared I2C SDA pin"
        default 9
        help
            Used by the rail monitor's OLED and by the I2C presence check in
            every other mode. Avoid GPIO19/20 (native USB), GPIO43/44 (UART0
            console), 26..37 (flash/PSRAM) and 0/3/45/46 (strapping).

    config I2C_SCL_GPIO
        int "Shared I2C SCL pin"
        default 8
        help
            See I2C_SDA_GPIO.

    config I2C_PROBE_ADDR
        hex "First I2C address to try"
        default 0x3C
        help
            The usual display addresses are 0x3C and 0x3D. The probe tries this
            one first and then sweeps 0x08..0x77, so this is only a hint.

    menu "Rail monitor (voltage display, no serial needed)"

        # Why this exists: on a board whose 3.3V rail collapses during the
        # radio's power-up, a serial console is the wrong instrument - the USB
        # bridge can drop off the bus at the same moment, and the host sees
        # "no port" rather than data. A locally driven OLED (+ optional 7-seg
        # display) keeps reporting the rail minimum across resets, which is
        # the one measurement that distinguishes "input sagged" from "the
        # regulator folded back".
        #
        # See PROTOCOL-3V3-INJECTION.md and EXPERIMENT-2026-10-02-BROWNOUT.md.

        config RAIL_MONITOR
            bool "Enable the rail monitor firmware"
            default n
            help
                Replaces the assistant's normal startup with a diagnostic
                monitor: samples the 3.3V rail through an ADC pin and shows
                current / 5 s minimum / lowest-since-boot / reset reason and
                reset count on an I2C OLED, refreshed 4x per second.

                The minimum is what matters. A brownout threshold of 2.44V
                says only that the rail reached it; the displayed minimum says
                how far it actually fell, and the reset reason says whether it
                was the detector or a total collapse.

                Turn this off to get the assistant back.

        config RAIL_ADC_GPIO
            int "ADC pin used to watch the rail"
            depends on RAIL_MONITOR
            range 1 10
            default 1
            help
                Must be an ADC1 pin (GPIO1-10 on the ESP32-S3). ADC2 is not
                usable while WiFi is active, so the ADC1 range is enforced.

                Wire: 3V3 --[R1]--+--[R2]-- GND, and the midpoint to this pin.
                Nothing is connected to this pin by default.

        config RAIL_DIVIDER_X100
            int "Divider ratio x100 (2 = half, 1 = direct)"
            depends on RAIL_MONITOR
            range 100 300
            default 200
            help
                The ADC input must stay below about 3.1V, so the rail has to
                be divided down. With R1 = R2 = 10k the ratio is 2.00, which
                maps a 3.3V rail to 1.65V - comfortably inside range and far
                enough from the limit that a rail overshoot cannot damage the
                pin.

                Set to 100 only if the rail can never exceed 3.1V and you
                accept that risk.

        config RAIL_OLED_XOFFSET
            int "OLED column offset (0 = SSD1306, 2 = SH1106)"
            depends on RAIL_MONITOR
            range 0 4
            default 0
            help
                Most 0.96 inch modules are SSD1306 and want 0. If the picture
                is shifted two columns to the right with a strip of noise down
                one edge, the controller is an SH1106 (132-column RAM) and
                wants 2.

        config RAIL_SCAN_I2C_PINS
            bool "Search for the display if the configured pins do not answer"
            depends on RAIL_MONITOR
            default y
            help
                Probes every plausible GPIO pair for an I2C device when
                RAIL_I2C_SDA/SCL do not find one, and reports the pair that
                worked. Costs a second or two at boot and saves a
                recompile-and-reflash cycle per wiring guess.

                Strapping pins (0/3/45/46), native USB (19/20), flash/PSRAM
                (26..37) and the console UART (43/44) are never probed.

    endmenu

    config BROWNOUT_DIAG_TRACE
        bool "BROWNOUT DIAGNOSTIC: trace where the reset lands"
        default n
        help
            Prints a numbered marker before each step of WiFi startup, so the
            point at which the board resets is visible in the boot log instead
            of having to be guessed.

            How to read the output. A marginal supply lets startup get only so
            far before the rail collapses, and the last marker printed is
            where it died:

                W (622) wifi: [1/6] nvs_flash_init  (WiFi STA startup)
                W (642) wifi: [2/6] esp_netif_init
                W (652) wifi: [3/6] esp_event_loop_create_default
                W (652) wifi: [4/6] esp_wifi_init (RF calibration)
                W (762) wifi: [5/6] esp_wifi_init returned
                W (782) wifi: [6/6] esp_wifi_start (first transmit)
                <reset here, no "[6/6] returned">

            Reaching [5/6] but never "[6/6] ... returned" means RF calibration
            completed and the reset is inside esp_wifi_start() - which is what
            this board does. Stopping earlier points at calibration or at the
            netif/event setup instead.

            Note the ordering: these markers are printed before and after the
            call they describe, so the last line seen identifies the call that
            did not finish.

            Why a switch rather than always-on logging: the markers are
            ESP_LOGW, so they print regardless of the configured log level,
            and on a healthy board they are pure noise in every boot. Turn
            this on when investigating, off otherwise.

            There is a companion script that does the capture and analysis:

                powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File .\trace-boot.ps1

    config SKIP_WIFI
        bool "BROWNOUT DIAGNOSTIC: do not start WiFi at all"
        default n
        help
            Bring-up aid. When set, the firmware initialises the LED, the
            recording buffer and both I2S peripherals, then idles printing a
            heartbeat - it never touches the WiFi radio.

            Use this to tell apart two failure modes that look identical:

              * stable with this option ON and resetting with it OFF
                  -> the rail collapses under the radio's current draw,
                     i.e. a genuine supply problem.

              * resetting either way
                  -> the fault is elsewhere (PSRAM, I2S, a pin conflict)
                     and the radio is merely the first thing loud enough
                     to trip it.

            Turn it back off once the diagnosis is made.

    config FORCE_DISABLE_BROWNOUT
        bool "WORKAROUND: force the brownout detector off at runtime"
        default n
        help
            The Kconfig option CONFIG_ESP_BROWNOUT_DET only stops IDF from
            *enabling* the detector during startup; it does not disable a
            detector that is already running, and on the ESP32-S3 the RF
            power-up path re-arms the PMU comparator regardless. This option
            calls esp_brownout_disable() explicitly, which is the only way to
            actually turn it off.

            READ THIS BEFORE ENABLING:

            A brownout reset means the supply really did dip below roughly
            2.4 V. Suppressing the reset does not fix the dip - it lets the
            chip keep running outside its specification. Two concrete risks:

              * a write to flash that is interrupted by a real voltage dip
                can corrupt the image, leaving a board that needs reflashing
              * the radio and PSRAM may misbehave in ways that are hard to
                attribute

            Treat this as a bring-up escape hatch so the rest of the pipeline
            can be exercised, not as a fix. The real remedy is a supply that
            holds the 3V3 rail up under the radio's current bursts.

endmenu
